   "page_count": 2,
   "pages": [
    {
     "page": 1,
     "text": "CLINICAL SUMMARY - Pace LLC Medical Group\n\nPatient: Rebecca Trujillo DOB: 06/23/2008 Sex: F MRN: 840604\n\nEncounters:\n 2026-01-23 NAFLD / fatty liver BP 120/72 Wt 117\n 2025-09-14 NAFLD / fatty liver BP 122/69 Wt 116\n 2025-05-06 NAFLD / fatty liver BP 117/68 Wt 129\n 2024-12-26 NAFLD / fatty liver BP 125/67 Wt 125\n 2024-08-16 NAFLD / fatty liver BP 115/66 Wt 129\n 2024-04-07 medication refill BP 123/62 Wt 114\n\nAssessment/Plan:\n • NAFLD / fatty liver (fibrosis F4) - controlled; meds: none\n\nRebecca Trujillo, a 17-year-old female, has been under my care for management of her non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), also known as fatty liver disease. She was diagnosed with NAFLD in June 2025, and the condition is currently controlled with no medications.\n\nHer initial diagnosis of NAFLD was confirmed through liver biopsy, which showed fibrosis at stage F4. The patient has been compliant with follow-up appointments, which have allowed us to monitor her condition closely. At her latest on January 23, 2026, her blood pressure was 120/72 and her weight was 117 pounds. She reported no new symptoms and continues to maintain a controlled condition.\n\nThroughout her care, we have focused on lifestyle modifications to manage her NAFLD. Rebecca has been counseled on the importance of a balanced diet and regular physical activity. Her weight has fluctuated slightly, with a range between 114 to 129 pounds over the past two years, but her most recent weight is 117 pounds. This is within a healthy range for her age and height. We have also discussed the importance of avoiding alcohol, given her diagnosis.\n\nIn addition to monitoring her liver health, we have reviewed other aspects of her overall blood pressure has remained stable and within normal limits during each visit, readings ranging from 115/66 to 123/62. Her most recent reading was 120/72 on January 23, 2026.\n\nSocial history is significant for a past history of tobacco use. Rebecca reported smoking approximately 1.5 cigarettes per day and started smoking on March 25, 2015. She quit smoking on April 28, 2016. She has been honest about her tobacco use, and we have provided resources for smoking cessation if needed.\n\nRebecca drinks alcohol on occasion, approximately 2 days per week. This is within moderate limits and has not affected her liver function as per the most recent lab results.\n\nHer family history is noteworthy for a maternal grandmother who was diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease at the age of 80 and passed away at age 82. There are no other significant m"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "edical conditions reported in her family history.\n\nIn summary, Rebecca Trujillo's NAFLD / fatty liver is well-controlled without the need for medication. She remains compliant with follow-up appointments and lifestyle recommendations. Her vital signs are stable, and she reports no new symptoms. Continued monitoring and management will be essential to maintain her controlled condition.\n\nDr. Patricia Wilson, MD\nGastroenterology\nPace LLC Medical Group\n"
    }
   ],
